# 2. Objective

The main objectives of this laboratory are:

- To understand the mathematical foundations of **Forward Kinematics**.
- To calculate the position and orientation of a robot's end-effector from its joint variables.
- To implement forward kinematics using homogeneous transformation matrices.
- To validate analytical calculations using numerical Python implementations.
- To use Robotics Toolbox for verification of kinematic transformations.
- To integrate forward kinematics with the MuJoCo physics engine.
- To visualize robot motion and end-effector coordinates in real time.
- To compare calculated kinematic results with the robot model simulated in MuJoCo.

---

# 3. Theoretical Background

## 3.1 Forward Kinematics

Forward Kinematics (FK) determines the position and orientation of a robot's end-effector with respect to a reference frame when the joint variables are known.

For a robotic manipulator with joint variables

$$
\boldsymbol{\theta}
=
[\theta_1,\theta_2,\ldots,\theta_n]^T
$$

the forward kinematic relationship can be represented as

$$
\mathbf{x} = f(\boldsymbol{\theta})
$$

where:

- $\boldsymbol{\theta}$ represents the joint angles or joint variables.
- $\mathbf{x}$ represents the Cartesian pose of the end-effector.
- $f(\cdot)$ represents the forward kinematic transformation.

For a spatial manipulator, the complete pose is generally represented using a homogeneous transformation matrix:

$$
T =
\begin{bmatrix}
R & p \\
0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
$$

where:

- $R$ is the $3\times3$ rotation matrix representing orientation.
- $p$ is the $3\times1$ position vector representing the end-effector location.

---

# 4. 2-DOF Planar Manipulator

For a planar robotic arm with two revolute joints and link lengths $L_1$ and $L_2$, the end-effector position can be obtained using basic trigonometry.

The Cartesian coordinates are:

$$
x =
L_1\cos(\theta_1)
+
L_2\cos(\theta_1+\theta_2)
$$

$$
y =
L_1\sin(\theta_1)
+
L_2\sin(\theta_1+\theta_2)
$$

where:

- $L_1$ = length of the first link.
- $L_2$ = length of the second link.
- $\theta_1$ = angle of the first joint relative to the base.
- $\theta_2$ = relative angle of the second joint.

The end-effector orientation for the planar manipulator is:

$$
\phi = \theta_1 + \theta_2
$$

Therefore, the complete planar pose can be represented as:

$$
\mathbf{x}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\
y\\
\phi
\end{bmatrix}
$$

---

# 5. Spatial Manipulator Kinematics

For spatial robotic manipulators such as the **6-DOF UR5e** and **7-DOF Franka Emika Panda**, the relationship between consecutive coordinate frames can be represented using homogeneous transformation matrices.

The Denavit-Hartenberg (DH) convention uses four parameters:

- $a_i$ — link length
- $\alpha_i$ — link twist
- $d_i$ — link offset
- $\theta_i$ — joint angle

The homogeneous transformation from frame $i-1$ to frame $i$ is:

$$
T_i^{i-1}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
\cos\theta_i &
-\sin\theta_i\cos\alpha_i &
\sin\theta_i\sin\alpha_i &
a_i\cos\theta_i
\\
\sin\theta_i &
\cos\theta_i\cos\alpha_i &
-\cos\theta_i\sin\alpha_i &
a_i\sin\theta_i
\\
0 &
\sin\alpha_i &
\cos\alpha_i &
d_i
\\
0 & 0 & 0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
$$

The complete forward kinematics transformation is obtained by multiplying the transformations of all individual joints:

$$
T_0^n
=
T_0^1
T_1^2
T_2^3
\cdots
T_{n-1}^n
$$

or equivalently, depending on the transformation notation convention:

$$
T_{\mathrm{total}}
=
T_1^0
T_2^1
T_3^2
\cdots
T_n^{n-1}
$$

The resulting matrix has the form:

$$
T_{\mathrm{total}}
=
\begin{bmatrix}
r_{11} & r_{12} & r_{13} & x\\
r_{21} & r_{22} & r_{23} & y\\
r_{31} & r_{32} & r_{33} & z\\
0 & 0 & 0 & 1
\end{bmatrix}
$$

where

$$
p =
\begin{bmatrix}
x\\
y\\
z
\end{bmatrix}
$$

represents the Cartesian position of the end-effector, while the upper-left $3\times3$ matrix represents its orientation.

---

# 6. Numerical Implementation

The project implements the forward kinematics calculations using Python.

## 6.1 NumPy Implementation

A from-scratch implementation using **NumPy** is used to:

- Construct rotation matrices.
- Construct homogeneous transformation matrices.
- Perform matrix multiplication.
- Propagate transformations from the base to the end-effector.
- Calculate the final Cartesian position and orientation.
- Validate the mathematical derivation independently of MuJoCo.

This provides a direct numerical implementation of the underlying kinematic equations.

---

## 6.2 Robotics Toolbox

The **Robotics Toolbox for Python** is used as an additional verification tool.

It is used for:

- Defining robot kinematic models.
- Calculating forward kinematics.
- Verifying homogeneous transformation matrices.
- Comparing analytical and numerical results.
- Visualizing robot configurations.

The toolbox provides an independent reference against which the manually implemented forward kinematics can be compared.

---

# 7. MuJoCo Physics Simulation

The project integrates the forward kinematics implementation with **MuJoCo**, a physics engine designed for robotics and articulated-body simulation.

The simulation uses XML-based robot descriptions to define:

- Robot links
- Joints
- Geometries
- Coordinate frames
- Actuators
- Environment objects
- Collision properties
- Physical parameters

The project uses models from the **MuJoCo Menagerie**, including:

- **Franka Emika Panda**
- **UR5e**

along with custom laboratory robot descriptions.

---

# 8. Coordinate Frames

The MuJoCo visualization provides coordinate-frame information for the robot.

The standard Cartesian coordinate convention is:

| Axis | Representation |
|---|---|
| X-axis | Red |
| Y-axis | Green |
| Z-axis | Blue |

These coordinate frames help visualize the orientation and position of the robot's links and end-effector.

---

# 9. Live End-Effector Tracking

The MuJoCo Python API provides access to the simulated state of the robot.

The end-effector position can be obtained from MuJoCo's body/site position data, such as:

```python
data.xpos
```

This allows the program to track the Cartesian position of the end-effector during simulation.

The simulation therefore provides a direct way to compare:

1. Joint angles entered into the robot.
2. Forward kinematics calculated numerically.
3. End-effector position obtained from MuJoCo.

This provides a practical validation of the forward kinematics implementation.

---

# 10. Interactive Simulation

The main simulation is implemented in:

```text
task_3_final.py
```

The script launches the MuJoCo viewer and allows the user to interact with the robot model.

The simulation provides:

- Interactive robot visualization.
- Joint-angle control.
- Real-time robot movement.
- End-effector Cartesian tracking.
- Live forward kinematics calculations.
- Terminal output of XYZ coordinates.
- Visualization of coordinate frames.

---

# 11. Robot Models

## 11.1 Franka Emika Panda

The **Franka Emika Panda** is a 7-DOF robotic manipulator.

It is used in the simulation to demonstrate forward kinematics for a redundant spatial manipulator.

The seven joint variables are used to determine the resulting end-effector pose.

---

## 11.2 UR5e

The **UR5e** is a 6-DOF industrial robotic manipulator.

It provides a practical example of spatial forward kinematics for a standard six-joint robotic arm.

The joint configuration is mapped to the end-effector pose through the robot's kinematic chain.

---

# 12. Software Requirements

The project requires:

- Python 3
- NumPy
- MuJoCo
- Robotics Toolbox for Python
- Matplotlib, if required by the visualization/robotics toolbox
- A working MuJoCo-compatible graphical environment

The recommended environment is an Ubuntu-based system with a Python or Conda virtual environment.

---

# 13. Environment Setup

## Using Conda

```bash
conda activate mujoco_env
```

## Using a Python Virtual Environment

```bash
source mujoco_env/bin/activate
```

Install the required packages if they are not already installed:

```bash
pip install numpy mujoco roboticstoolbox-python matplotlib
```

---

# 14. Running the Simulation

Navigate to the project directory:

```bash
cd Lab_29_09_2026
```

Activate the environment:

```bash
conda activate mujoco_env
```

Run the main simulation:

```bash
python task_3_final.py
```

The MuJoCo viewer should open and display the selected robot model.

---

# 15. Using the Simulation

After launching the program:

1. Select the required robot model.
2. Use the available control sliders to change the joint angles.
3. Observe the corresponding robot motion in the MuJoCo viewer.
4. Monitor the end-effector coordinate values printed in the terminal.
5. Compare the simulated position with the forward kinematics calculation.
6. Observe the coordinate frames attached to the robot.

The available robot selection is:

```text
1 → Franka Emika Panda
2 → UR5e
```

---

# 16. Validation Approach

The implementation can be validated through three different approaches.

## Analytical Calculation

The forward kinematics equations are derived using trigonometric relationships or homogeneous transformation matrices.

## Numerical Python Implementation

The analytical equations are implemented using NumPy to calculate the transformation matrix and end-effector pose.

## MuJoCo Simulation

The resulting robot configuration is simulated in MuJoCo and the corresponding end-effector position is obtained directly from the simulation state.

The three approaches can then be compared to verify the correctness of the implementation.

---

# 17. Project Workflow

The overall workflow of the project is:

```text
                Joint Angles
                     │
                     ▼
        Forward Kinematics Equations
                     │
                     ▼
       Homogeneous Transformation
               Matrices
                     │
                     ▼
         NumPy Numerical
           Implementation
                     │
              ┌──────┴──────┐
              ▼             ▼
     Robotics Toolbox      MuJoCo
              │             │
              ▼             ▼
      FK Verification   Physics Simulation
              │             │
              └──────┬──────┘
                     ▼
             End-Effector Pose
                (X, Y, Z, R)
```

---

# 18. Expected Output

When the simulation is running, changing the joint angles should result in corresponding changes in the robot configuration and end-effector position.

The terminal provides Cartesian position information similar to:

```text
End Effector Position:
X = ...
Y = ...
Z = ...
```

The MuJoCo viewer simultaneously displays the physical robot configuration.

The calculated values can be compared with the position obtained from the forward kinematics transformation.

